  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Tuesday) \nIssued at 1256 AM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nDry weather is forecast Saturday, and most places on Sunday as\nwell, as an upper level trough tracking eastward over the\nUS/Canadian border steers much drier air over NM. High\ntemperatures will continue to warm, reaching the 70s and 80s by\nSunday; except for 60s and some 50s in the mountains. Showers and\nthunderstorms will increase in coverage over southwest areas\nSunday afternoon, then west of the central mountain chain Sunday\nnight, and areawide on Monday into Tuesday, as an upper level\ntrough crossing the western US draws rich subtropical moisture\nnorthward again. On Monday, GEFS Ensemble PWATs look to peak\nbetween 170-200% of normal, or from 1-1.28 inches, enabling a \nreturn of locally heavy rain at rates over 1 inch per hour; as\nwell as a risk of flash flooding that will probably linger into \nTuesday (at least in the east). With the increasing moisture early\nin the coming work week, high temperatures will trend downward\nagain.",
